# Whatever env vars the configured model provider needs, credentials
# included. Copy to provider.env, which is not in git:
#
#     cp configs/env/provider.env.example configs/env/provider.env
#
# Read only by the three services that call a model: extraction, topic
# modelling and question generation. The api is given the model settings and
# never these, because it sends no request to the model.
#
# litellm reads whichever its own provider needs, so nothing here is named by
# this project. Leave the file empty for a plain Ollama, which needs none of
# it. Which provider is used is not set here at all - it is the prefix of
# LLM_MODEL in .env.
#
#     LLM_MODEL=ollama_chat/gemma4:31b       a local Ollama, no key
#     LLM_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o                OPENAI_API_KEY
#     LLM_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5  ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
#     LLM_MODEL=gemini/gemini-2.5-pro        GEMINI_API_KEY
#     LLM_MODEL=hosted_vllm/<model>          HOSTED_VLLM_API_KEY
#     LLM_MODEL=bedrock/<model>              AWS_* below
#     LLM_MODEL=azure/<deployment>           AZURE_* below
#
# `make doctor` asks the configured model one question and reports what it
# answered, which is where a wrong key or an unreachable address surfaces.

# ── A hosted provider with one key ────────────────────────────────────────
# Uncomment the one that matches LLM_MODEL's prefix.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# HOSTED_VLLM_API_KEY=

# ── Ollama behind a gateway ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# A plain Ollama needs none of this.
# OLLAMA_API_KEY=

# ── Bedrock ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# AWS_REGION_NAME=

# ── Azure ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# With LLM_MODEL=azure/<deployment>, where the deployment name is the one in
# the Foundry resource rather than the model's own name.
AZURE_API_VERSION=2024-12-01-preview
# AZURE_API_BASE=https://<resource>.openai.azure.com

# A key, if that is how this deployment authenticates.
# AZURE_API_KEY=

# ── Azure with Entra ID instead of a key ──────────────────────────────────
#
# Needs no key variable at all, and the choice is really about WHERE the
# process runs. Two-factor happens when a token is ISSUED, not when it is
# used, so a container never does the 2FA itself: it is either given an
# identity that has none, or lent the result of a 2FA somebody already did.
#
# Four ways, in the order litellm tries them.
#
# 1. A SERVICE PRINCIPAL, and the right answer for a container. An app
#    identity is not a user, so no phone is involved and nothing expires in
#    an hour. Needs an app registration, and the `Cognitive Services OpenAI
#    User` role granted to it on the Foundry resource.
# AZURE_TENANT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# 2. MANAGED or WORKLOAD IDENTITY, through DefaultAzureCredential, which
#    needs nothing set here. Only on Azure infrastructure - AKS, Container
#    Apps, a VM. On a laptop the probe to 169.254.169.254 simply times out.
#
# 3. `az login` ON THE HOST, also through DefaultAzureCredential and also
#    nothing set here. You do the 2FA once, in a browser, and the CLI keeps
#    the refresh token in ~/.azure. **This is why a stage is run from the
#    host rather than in a container**: `make extract`, `make topics` and
#    `make questions` inherit that session, and the Makefile now sources
#    this file for them.
#
#    A container can use it too, but only by being handed both halves: bind
#    -mount ~/.azure AND install the az CLI in the image, because
#    AzureCliCredential works by shelling out to `az`. The image carries no
#    az today, which is what makes this the host's path.
#
# 4. A TOKEN MINTED BY HAND, host-side, which expires in about an hour:
#
#     az account get-access-token --resource https://cognitiveservices.azure.com \
#       --query accessToken -o tsv
#
#    The way to lend a container one 2FA without mounting anything, and the
#    only credential here that goes stale on its own. Leave it commented
#    rather than leaving a stale value: an empty string and an expired token
#    are both taken in preference to a working credential, and every call is
#    then refused. `make doctor` reads its `exp` and says so by name.
# AZURE_OPENAI_AD_TOKEN=
#
# A scope, only if the endpoint is a gateway that wants something other than
# https://cognitiveservices.azure.com/.default
# AZURE_SCOPE=
